---
title: 'Series Labels'
description: '$framework Charts support styling series data labels, controlling their position and orientation, and avoiding collisions between labels, markers and other series geometry.'
---

Series data labels display the value of a data point directly on the chart, alongside or instead of a [Tooltip](./tooltips/). Labels are configured on the `label` property of each series.

Please see the [API Reference](#api-reference) for the full list of available options, which vary slightly between series types.

## Styling

{% chartExampleRunner title="Label Styling" name="label-showcase" type="generated" /%}

Enable labels with `label.enabled`, then style them with the following options.

```js format="snippet"
{
    series: [
        {
            // ...
            label: {
                enabled: true,
            },
        },
    ],
}
```

- Text is styled with `color`, `fontSize`, `fontWeight`, `fontStyle` and `fontFamily`.
- The label itself is filled and bordered with `fill`, `fillOpacity`, `cornerRadius`, `padding` and `border` — see [Fills & Borders](./fills-borders/) for these options in the wider context of chart styling.
- Bar-family labels can additionally be rotated with `orientation` — see [Orientation](#orientation) below.
- Providing `placement` as an ordered array lets a label fall back to an alternative position — see [Position](#position) below.

## Position

Where a label sits relative to its data point is series-specific — for example `'inside-start'`, `'outside-end'` or a `'beside-*'` value for a bar, or a directional value like `'top'` or `'left'` for a line or bubble. Provide `placement` as an ordered array to fall back to an alternative position if the first doesn't fit. See the [API Reference](#api-reference) for the full list of placement values per series type.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Label Position" name="label-position" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    series: [
        {
            // ...
            label: {
                enabled: true,
                placement: ['top', 'bottom', 'left', 'right'],
                spacing: 6,
            },
        },
    ],
}
```

In this example:

- `spacing` sets the pixel distance between a label and its anchor. It has no effect when the resolved placement is centred, since the label sits on top of its anchor rather than offset from it.
- Use `insideStyle` and `outsideStyle` to vary a label's colour, fill or border depending on which placement is resolved — shown in the example at the top of this page.
- A fallback candidate is only tried once the current one collides with an obstacle, and the resolved placement also depends on the collision threshold — see [Collision Avoidance](#collision-avoidance) below. Try switching series type and resizing the example to trigger a fallback.

## Orientation

Bar-family series can rotate their label within its segment with `label.orientation`, accepting `'horizontal'`, `'vertical'` or `'vertical-reversed'`, or an ordered array of fallback orientations tried in turn.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Label Orientation" name="label-orientation" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    series: [
        {
            type: 'bar',
            // ...
            label: {
                enabled: true,
                orientation: ['horizontal', 'vertical'],
            },
        },
    ],
}
```

In this example:

- Orientation fallback uses the same collision mechanism as placement, and is also affected by the collision threshold — see [Collision Avoidance](#collision-avoidance) below.
- Try resizing the example to trigger a fallback.

## Collision Avoidance

A label falls back to the next candidate in an ordered `placement` (or, for bar-family series, `orientation`) array when it collides with another label, a marker, or series geometry — or when it doesn't fit within `maxWidth`/`maxHeight`. Each candidate is tried in turn until one is found clear of obstacles.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Label Fitting" name="label-fitting" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    series: [
        {
            // ...
            label: {
                enabled: true,
                placement: ['top', 'bottom', 'left', 'right'],
                maxWidth: 70,
                wrapping: 'on-space',
                truncate: true,
            },
        },
    ],
}
```

In this example:

- `maxWidth` and `maxHeight` bound the region a label is fitted to; `wrapping` (`'on-space'`, `'always'`, `'hyphenate'`, `'never'`) controls how overflowing text wraps within that region, and `truncate` ellipsis-truncates whatever still doesn't fit. Only bar-family series honour these options.
- Bar-family labels are never hidden by collision avoidance — instead they fall back to whichever candidate position overlaps the least.

Series label collision avoidance is separate from [axis label collision avoidance](./axes-labels/#collision-avoidance), which is configured independently on each axis.

### Threshold

{% chartExampleRunner title="Label Collision Threshold" name="label-threshold" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    series: [
        {
            // ...
            label: {
                enabled: true,
                collision: {
                    threshold: 4,
                },
            },
        },
    ],
}
```

In this example, `collision.threshold` is the minimum pixel spacing kept from an obstacle before it counts as a collision, shown as a border around each label. A negative value allows labels to overlap without triggering fallback; raising it forces fallback to trigger sooner.

### Hiding Labels

{% chartExampleRunner title="Label Collision Threshold" name="label-threshold" type="generated" /%}

```js format="snippet"
{
    series: [
        {
            // ...
            label: {
                enabled: true,
                collision: {
                    suppressHide: true,
                },
            },
        },
    ],
}
```

In this example, point-based series hide a label that collision avoidance cannot place clear of its obstacles by default. Set `collision.suppressHide` to keep it visible instead, trading label completeness for visibility — a label kept this way may overlap other chart elements.

